          "rendered_sha256": "57e4502e20e0aad0de0e76099845664f52257fb1fb103f86721d59259737d363"
        }
      ],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14243614/observations/step-q334872-04d525104018f937ca22.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14243614/observations/step-q334872-04d525104018f937ca22.png",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-xy)=2-4x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-4x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-4(0.5)-(1) & -(0.5) \\\\ 4(1) & -2+4(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.5\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$",
      "can_dont_know": false,
      "can_skip": false,
      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-q334872",
        "name": "Question 1",
        "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.5,\\,1]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"40px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"40px\" align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}.",
        "worked_solution": "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-xy)=2-4x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-4x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-4(0.5)-(1) & -(0.5) \\\\ 4(1) & -2+4(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.5\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$",
