      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14243614/observations/step-q335594-d392a2de7aecde7e4ab4.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14243614/observations/step-q335594-d392a2de7aecde7e4ab4.png",
      "worked_solution": "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.1x)-xy=0 \\\\ -3y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-3+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=3.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.1x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.1x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(10,0).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)(1-0.1(3))-(3)y & =0 \\\\ 4.2-3y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(3,1.4).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(10,0),\\,(3,1.4).\n$$",
      "can_dont_know": false,
      "can_skip": false,
      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-q335594",
        "name": "Question 3",
        "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.1x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$",
        "worked_solution": "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.1x)-xy=0 \\\\ -3y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-3+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=3.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.1x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.1x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(10,0).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)(1-0.1(3))-(3)y & =0 \\\\ 4.2-3y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(3,1.4).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(10,0),\\,(3,1.4).\n$$",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
