/mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c35\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-cB1\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mtext class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c69\"></mjx-c></mjx-mtext></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>±</mo><mtext>i</mtext></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></div></div></div>.<p></p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"133\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\" tabindex=\"0\"><mjx-math aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\"><mjx-mstyle><mjx-mspace style=\"width: 1em;\"></mjx-mspace></mjx-mstyle></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\"><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace></mstyle></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> Therefore, the equilibrium at <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"134\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\" tabindex=\"0\"><mjx-math aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\"><mjx-msup><mjx-texatom texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-b\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D431 TEX-B\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-texatom><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: 0.363em;\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2217\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-script></mjx-msup></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> is \n<div class=\"selectList\" id=\"selectList-334872-5\" style=\"margin: 0px 4px 2px;\"><div class=\"selectListFrame correctSelection\" id=\"selectListFrame-334872-5\" style=\"width: 177px; height: 17px; line-height: 17px;\">an asymptotically stable</div><div class=\"selectListOptions\" style=\"visibility: hidden; z-index: 1;\"><div class=\"selectListOption\" style=\"height: 17px;\">an asymptotically stable</div><div class=\"selectListOption\" style=\"height: 17px;\">an unstable</div><div class=\"selectListOption\" style=\"height: 17px;\">a stable</div></div></div><div class=\"selectList\" id=\"selectList-334872-6\" style=\"margin: 0px 2px 2px 4px;\"><div class=\"selectListFrame correctSelection\" id=\"selectListFrame-334872-6\" style=\"width: 103px; height: 17px; line-height: 17px;\">spiral sink</div><div class=\"selectListOptions\" style=\"visibility: hidden; z-index: 1;\"><div class=\"selectListOption\" style=\"height: 17px;\">spiral sink</div><div class=\"selectListOption\" style=\"height: 17px;\">saddle</div><div class=\"selectListOption\" style=\"height: 17px;\">spiral source</div><div class=\"selectListOption\" style=\"height: 17px;\">center</div></div></div>.\n</span></div><div class=\"answerDetails\"><div class=\"questionDifficulty difficultyHard\">H</div><span class=\"answerCreated\">Thu, Oct 15th @ 2:52 PM</span><span class=\"answerDetailsSeparator\">|</span><span class=\"timeElpased\">Elapsed: 0:50</span><span class=\"answerResult\"><span style=\"color: green;\">Full Credit</span></span></div></div>"
      },
      {
        "dom_id": "question-334877",
        "name": null,
        "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-4x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"40px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"40px\" align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}.",
        "worked_solution": "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-4x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-4x)-xy)=2-16x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-4x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-16x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-16(0)-(0) & -(0) \\\\ 4(0) & -2+4(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
